# sqlstyle.guide (TransLution fork) -- the one ignore file for this repository.
#
# Read before adding a rule. An ignore rule never untracks a file, and git
# still reports changes to tracked files it matches. What a rule DOES do is
# hide NEW files under it: `git status` stops showing them and `git add`
# needs -f. Check what a rule matches with
#     git ls-files -i -c --exclude-standard        # must print nothing
#
# The upstream block at the bottom is Simon Holywell's, unchanged. The TransLution
# blocks sit above it so an upstream sync touches only the lines below the marker.

# --- build output -------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output for all projects
# Estate standard, as in TransLutionServices. This repository has no .NET
# projects today (0 tracked files under bin/ or obj/), so the rule hides nothing.
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# --- editor and IDE state -------------------------------------------------------
.vs/
.vscode/
*.suo
*.user
Desktop.ini

# --- tool output ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jekyll's own output (_site, .jekyll-metadata) is ignored in the upstream block.
# Ruby gems installed locally with `bundle install --path vendor/bundle`:
vendor/
.bundle/
# direnv's cache for the Nix flake (.envrc is tracked; its state is not):
.direnv/

# --- reference/ ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Extracted data must never be committed. This repository holds no customer data
# and nothing extracts any today; the rule is the estate standard, kept so it is
# already in place if that changes.
reference/_data/modules/

# ===== upstream (treffynnon/sqlstyle.guide) -- do not edit below this line =====
nbproject
.ruby-version
*.orig
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
Gemfile.lock

_site
_gh_pages
.jekyll-metadata
